1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,024
This is the worked example for practicals one and two:

2
00:00:04,024 --> 00:00:08,122
exploratory data analysis on California housing prices.

3
00:00:08,122 --> 00:00:17,808
We use twenty thousand six hundred forty districts and ten columns, and we compare what the numbers say with what the graphs show.

4
00:00:18,608 --> 00:00:19,618
The question:

5
00:00:19,618 --> 00:00:22,416
do the numbers and the graphs agree?

6
00:00:22,416 --> 00:00:27,701
What do a district's housing characteristics look like on their own:

7
00:00:27,701 --> 00:00:33,841
symmetric or skewed, tight or spread out, and how many outlier districts exist?

8
00:00:33,841 --> 00:00:35,706
The plan has five steps:

9
00:00:35,706 --> 00:00:50,240
clean the data, compute descriptive statistics with outlier counts, draw histogram, boxplot and density plots, build pivot tables, and finally compare statistical and graphical inference.

10
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:53,745
We load the file and clean it first.

11
00:00:53,745 --> 00:01:05,690
There are zero duplicates, but two hundred seven districts are missing total bedrooms, so we drop them, leaving twenty thousand four hundred thirty three rows.

12
00:01:05,690 --> 00:01:14,030
We also check for zero population, households or rooms, which would be physically impossible data entry errors.

13
00:01:14,030 --> 00:01:20,416
Cleaning first matters because these rows would distort every statistic that follows.

14
00:01:21,216 --> 00:01:28,124
The dependent variable is median house value, the outcome a buyer or seller cares about.

15
00:01:28,124 --> 00:01:35,347
The independent variables are median income, housing median age, total rooms and population.

16
00:01:35,347 --> 00:01:44,218
Note that rooms, population and households all measure district size and are strongly correlated with each other.

17
00:01:44,218 --> 00:01:50,184
Ocean proximity is a categorical feature we use to segment the pivot tables.

18
00:01:50,984 --> 00:01:58,836
For each feature we compute the mean, median, variance, I Q R, outlier fences, skewness and kurtosis.

19
00:01:58,836 --> 00:02:02,412
Median income is right-skewed and leptokurtic.

20
00:02:02,412 --> 00:02:11,664
The mean is nine point five percent above the median, skewness is one point six four and kurtosis four point nine four.

21
00:02:11,664 --> 00:02:20,216
There are six hundred seventy outliers, three point two eight percent, and they are the high-income districts.

22
00:02:21,016 --> 00:02:23,785
This is the code behind the charts.

23
00:02:23,785 --> 00:02:25,684
One row of three frames:

24
00:02:25,684 --> 00:02:39,372
a histogram showing how often values occur, a sideways boxplot showing the median, middle fifty percent and outliers, and a smoothed density curve showing the overall shape.

25
00:02:39,372 --> 00:02:42,616
We reuse the same code for every feature.

26
00:02:43,416 --> 00:02:47,138
Housing median age is the best-behaved feature.

27
00:02:47,138 --> 00:03:02,026
The mean is only one point three percent below the median, skewness is zero point zero six, so it is near-symmetric, and kurtosis is negative zero point eight, flatter than a normal curve.

28
00:03:02,026 --> 00:03:05,352
There are zero outliers by the I Q R rule.

29
00:03:06,152 --> 00:03:11,034
Total rooms is strongly right-skewed and extremely leptokurtic.

30
00:03:11,034 --> 00:03:20,411
The mean is twenty four percent above the median, skewness is four point one six and kurtosis thirty two point seven one.

31
00:03:20,411 --> 00:03:26,146
Twelve hundred ninety outliers, over six percent, are the large districts.

32
00:03:26,146 --> 00:03:29,168
This is the most outlier-heavy feature.

33
00:03:29,968 --> 00:03:32,549
Population is the most extreme.

34
00:03:32,549 --> 00:03:41,540
Skewness is almost five, and kurtosis is seventy four, a very sharp peak with rare but very large districts.

35
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:48,616
Eleven hundred ninety outliers sit above the upper whisker, mostly dense urban areas.

36
00:03:49,416 --> 00:03:55,281
Median house value, our dependent variable, is right-skewed but only moderately.

37
00:03:55,281 --> 00:04:03,785
The mean is fifteen percent above the median, skewness is zero point nine eight and kurtosis zero point three three.

38
00:04:03,785 --> 00:04:14,928
The one thousand sixty four outliers are the highest-value districts, matching the long tail past four hundred eighty thousand dollars in the histogram.

39
00:04:15,728 --> 00:04:18,146
Reading the whole table together:

40
00:04:18,146 --> 00:04:26,427
housing median age is best behaved, population is the most extreme, and total rooms has the highest outlier rate.

41
00:04:26,427 --> 00:04:34,928
The dependent variable sits in the middle, clearly skewed but less extreme than the raw size measures that drive it.

42
00:04:35,728 --> 00:04:38,738
In practical two we build pivot tables.

43
00:04:38,738 --> 00:04:41,749
Average house value by ocean proximity:

44
00:04:41,749 --> 00:04:47,229
inland is the cheapest, roughly half the value of any coastal category.

45
00:04:47,229 --> 00:04:56,955
Island looks highest at three hundred eighty thousand, but there are only five such districts, far too few to generalise from.

46
00:04:56,955 --> 00:05:04,288
Among well-populated categories, near bay and near ocean outrank less than one hour from ocean.

47
00:05:05,088 --> 00:05:06,996
Now add income brackets.

48
00:05:06,996 --> 00:05:13,197
Within every ocean proximity category, house value rises steadily with income.

49
00:05:13,197 --> 00:05:22,977
Inland districts climb from about seventy eight thousand dollars to three hundred seventy nine thousand, nearly five times.

50
00:05:22,977 --> 00:05:27,190
Location sets a baseline and income scales it on top:

51
00:05:27,190 --> 00:05:29,496
the two effects are additive.

52
00:05:30,296 --> 00:05:32,028
The overall conclusion:

53
00:05:32,028 --> 00:05:37,828
for all five features, the numeric summary and the plots tell the same story.

54
00:05:37,828 --> 00:05:46,112
Population and total rooms show it most clearly, with extreme kurtosis and a sharp peak with a long thin tail.

55
00:05:46,112 --> 00:05:55,376
Agreement is a property of this dataset, not a given, which is the practical case for always pairing statistics with plots.

56
00:05:56,176 --> 00:05:57,158
To conclude:

57
00:05:57,158 --> 00:06:10,657
five features were fully profiled with mean, median, variance, standard deviation, I Q R, skewness, kurtosis and outlier counts, then cross-checked with three plots.

58
00:06:10,657 --> 00:06:16,875
Ocean proximity and income are independent, additive drivers of house value.

59
00:06:16,875 --> 00:06:21,948
And statistical and graphical views should always be compared.

60
00:06:21,948 --> 00:06:23,584
Thanks for watching.

